#ifndef BENCH_PROMPTS_H
#define BENCH_PROMPTS_H

namespace prompts {

inline const char* kEssay = R"PROMPT(In recent decades, artificial intelligence has progressed from a niche academic curiosity to a force that quietly reshapes nearly every corner of modern life. Algorithms now translate languages in real time, diagnose diseases from medical images, draft emails on our behalf, and pilot vehicles through unpredictable city streets. What once required entire research teams can sometimes be done by a single model running on a laptop. Yet despite these remarkable advances, the deeper questions remain unresolved: how should we measure understanding, what responsibilities accompany increasingly autonomous systems, and what role will human judgment play in a world where machines write, decide, and predict at unprecedented speed and scale? The next chapter of this story is still being written, and the choices we make in the coming years may depend less on any single breakthrough than on the institutions we build around the technology itself. Universities are redesigning their curricula to teach students not only how to train models but how to question them, and regulators on several continents are drafting rules that attempt to balance innovation against harm without freezing progress in place. The companies that deploy these systems face a quieter but equally difficult task: deciding which decisions should never be delegated to software, no matter how accurate it becomes.

History offers imperfect but useful guidance. When electricity spread through cities at the end of the nineteenth century, it took decades for factories to reorganize themselves around the new power source, and the largest productivity gains arrived only after managers stopped treating dynamos as drop-in replacements for steam engines. Artificial intelligence appears to be following a similar path. The first wave of adoption simply automated existing workflows, replacing a form with a chatbot or a checklist with a classifier. The second wave, now underway, is more structural: entire processes are being redesigned so that machines handle the routine center of the distribution while people concentrate on the exceptions, the edge cases, and the moments where context matters more than pattern. This division of labor sounds tidy in a keynote presentation, but in practice the boundary between routine and exception is blurry, contested, and constantly moving as models improve.

Consider medicine, where the stakes make every design decision visible. A radiology model that flags probable tumors can scan thousands of images without fatigue, and in controlled studies it often matches or exceeds the accuracy of experienced specialists. Yet accuracy on a benchmark is not the same as usefulness in a hospital. The model must fit into a workflow where physicians are already overloaded, where a false alarm consumes scarce attention, and where a missed lesion can end a life. Hospitals that succeeded with these tools did not simply install software; they rewrote protocols, retrained staff, and created new roles for people who audit the machine's output. The lesson generalizes far beyond health care: the technical model is a small fraction of the total system, and the surrounding human scaffolding usually determines whether the whole arrangement helps or harms.

Education tells a parallel story with a different emotional register. Students discovered generative models almost immediately, and teachers found themselves refereeing a contest they had not agreed to join. Some schools banned the tools outright, others embraced them as tutors, and most settled into an uneasy middle where policy lags practice by a semester or two. The deeper question is not whether students will use machine assistance — they will — but what kinds of thinking schools should certify once fluent prose can be generated on demand. Writing has long served as both a medium of communication and a proxy for thought; when the medium becomes cheap, institutions must decide what the proxy was really measuring. Early experiments suggest that oral examinations, project portfolios, and in-class reasoning exercises can fill part of the gap, though each carries costs in time and fairness that schools are only beginning to tally.

The labor market absorbs these shifts unevenly. Occupations built around producing standard documents, summarizing meetings, or answering routine questions are already feeling pressure, while trades that combine physical dexterity with on-the-spot judgment remain largely untouched. Economists debate whether the current wave will mirror past technological transitions, in which displaced workers eventually found new and often better roles, or whether the speed of this transition will outpace the institutions that retrain and redeploy people. Both camps agree on one point: the distribution of gains depends on policy choices, not just on the technology's raw capability. Tax codes, licensing rules, educational subsidies, and labor protections will shape whether productivity improvements translate into broad prosperity or concentrate into a narrow set of firms and regions.

Meanwhile, the research frontier keeps moving. Models that once handled only text now reason across images, audio, code, and structured data, and the boundary between perception and action is dissolving as systems begin to operate software, control laboratory equipment, and coordinate with one another. Each capability raises the ceiling of what automation can attempt and simultaneously widens the surface where things can go wrong. A system that merely drafts a paragraph can embarrass its user; a system that executes transactions, modifies infrastructure, or negotiates on someone's behalf can cause harm that is difficult to reverse. Engineering practice is adapting accordingly, borrowing ideas from aviation and nuclear safety: layered defenses, human confirmation for irreversible steps, extensive logging, and the institutional habit of investigating near misses rather than celebrating that nothing quite broke.

Trust, in the end, may prove to be the scarcest resource of the entire transition. People extend trust to institutions slowly, through repeated interactions in which promises are kept and errors are acknowledged, and they withdraw it quickly when either condition fails. Systems that explain their reasoning, expose their uncertainty, and fail loudly rather than silently give their operators a chance to build that kind of durable confidence. Systems that present confident fabrications with perfect grammar erode it for everyone, including for the careful builders who took the slower path. This is why transparency requirements, evaluation standards, and incident reporting are not bureaucratic decoration; they are the plumbing through which public confidence either flows or leaks away.

None of this diminishes the genuine wonder of the underlying science. That a mathematical function, trained to predict the next fragment of text, can compose poetry, debug programs, and tutor a child in algebra remains astonishing to the very researchers who built it. The honest position holds both truths at once: these systems are remarkable, and they are unfinished. They compress the written record of human knowledge into a form that can converse, and they inherit that record's errors, biases, and blind spots along with its brilliance. Treating them as oracles misunderstands what they are; treating them as toys underestimates what they can do. The craft lies in the middle, in building sociotechnical systems where machine speed and human judgment each cover the other's weaknesses.

So the question that opened this essay returns in a more practical form. The choices ahead are not primarily about whether to adopt artificial intelligence — that decision has largely been made by markets and momentum — but about how to adopt it in ways that keep people meaningfully in charge of the outcomes that matter. That work is distributed across parliaments and product teams, classrooms and courtrooms, standards bodies and startup boards. It is slower than the technology, less glamorous than the demos, and more consequential than either. The societies that navigate this transition well will not necessarily be the ones with the largest models or the fastest chips, but the ones that learned, through patient institutional experiment, how to harness extraordinary capability without surrendering ordinary accountability. If there is a single measure worth watching over the coming decade, it is not benchmark scores or parameter counts, but the growing or shrinking gap between what these systems can do and what we can collectively verify, govern, and correct. Closing that gap is the quiet project of our era, pursued not in a single dramatic act but in thousands of small decisions about design, deployment, and oversight — decisions that will determine whether the story of this technology is ultimately written by it, or by us. The work begins with an honest inventory of what we know, continues through the humility to admit what we do not, and matures into institutions that can absorb surprises without abandoning their principles along the way, year after difficult year.
 Consider, finally, the view from an ordinary household a decade from now. The assistant that schedules appointments and files taxes will feel as unremarkable as electric light, and children will find it strange that documents were ever composed without a machine's first draft. Beneath that surface of convenience, the household will be participating, mostly invisibly, in the institutional arrangements described above: consent frameworks governing what the assistant may remember, audit trails recording what it did on the family's behalf, and liability rules deciding who answers when it errs. Whether those arrangements feel protective or oppressive will depend on design decisions being made right now, in standards committees and product reviews that few will ever read about. Convenience always arrives first and asks questions later; the task of this generation of builders is to make sure the questions are at least asked on schedule. There is reason for guarded optimism. Previous generations domesticated technologies of comparable reach — the automobile, the telephone, the power grid — and did so with cruder tools of governance than we possess today. The difference this time is speed: capabilities that once took a generation to diffuse now spread in months, compressing the interval in which norms can form. That compression is the true novelty of the moment, more than any particular capability, and it explains why so much of the serious work now happens at the boundary between disciplines, where engineers learn the vocabulary of law, lawyers learn the limits of models, and both learn patience with each other. If that mutual education continues, the systems of the coming decade may yet earn the trust they will certainly demand, and the story that began with a simple prediction engine will be remembered not for what the machines learned about us, but for what we finally learned about ourselves.
 None of these lessons will be learned automatically, and none of the institutions described here will build themselves; they will be assembled, argued over, and revised by people who chose to treat this transition as their generation's shared responsibility rather than someone else's distant problem, one meeting, one standard, and one carefully examined failure at a time.
 Perhaps the most honest summary is that the technology has outrun our vocabulary for it. We still reach for words like intelligence, understanding, and intention because they are the words we have, not because they fit. Better ones will come, as they did for electricity and for flight, once enough people have lived alongside these systems long enough to notice what they actually do rather than what they seem to promise. Until then, the most useful posture is neither awe nor dismissal but patient attention: measuring carefully, publishing what we find, and keeping the comparison fair enough that the next reader can trust it without having to take our word.)PROMPT";

} // namespace prompts

#endif // BENCH_PROMPTS_H
